# Explore Dense Sumset Avoidance

A dense set can contain many sums without containing a complete prescribed sumset. This interactive path begins with one exact count, builds the union bound, and ends with an explicit search procedure. For background, see [sumsets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumset), the [probabilistic method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probabilistic_method), and the [union bound](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boole%27s_inequality).

## 1. The exponential price of containment

Choose the ambient size and pattern size. Every additional required point halves the fraction of possible supersets.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact counting argument</summary>
A superset of a fixed pattern $T$ is uniquely obtained by choosing an arbitrary subset of $U\setminus T$. There are $2^{|U|-|T|}$ choices. This is an equality, not an estimate.
</details>

{{demo:0}}

## 2. Overlapping bad events

The union bound safely adds individual containment costs even when configurations overlap. Overlap causes overcounting, so it improves rather than threatens the estimate.

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the dense finite avoidance theorem</summary>
For $|U|=N$, if $\sum_{j<d}\binom Nj+\sum_{T\in\mathcal F}2^{N-|T|}<2^N$, then some $S\subseteq U$ has $|S|\ge d$ and contains no $T\in\mathcal F$. The two sums count, respectively, subsets that are too small and an upper bound for subsets containing a forbidden pattern.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## 3. Addition forces growth

Experiment with two integer sets. Their sumset always has at least $|A|+|B|-1$ elements when both sets are nonempty.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the sharp growth proof</summary>
List $A=\{a_1<\cdots<a_r\}$ and $B=\{b_1<\cdots<b_s\}$. The chain $a_1+b_1<\cdots<a_r+b_1<a_r+b_2<\cdots<a_r+b_s$ supplies $r+s-1$ distinct sums.
</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

## 4. Spend the avoidance budget

The normalized sufficient condition is a lower binomial tail plus the sumset-containment budget. Adjust the parameters and watch when the certificate succeeds.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the additive theorem</summary>
If every prescribed pair has nonempty summands of size at least $k$ and its sumset lies in $U$, then each sumset has size at least $2k-1$. Therefore $\sum_{j<d}\binom Nj+|\mathcal P|2^{N-(2k-1)}<2^N$ guarantees a set of size at least $d$ containing none of the prescribed sumsets.
</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## 5. Beyond the raw union bound

The exact finite mechanism is complete, but asymptotic logarithmic thresholds require compressing many similar pairs into shared fingerprints. Explore [additive combinatorics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Additive_combinatorics) to see how small sumsets signal structure. The next challenge is to retain enough sums for a strong exponential penalty while using few enough fingerprints to keep the family manageable.

